  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a bascule bridge?",
      "a": "A bascule bridge has one or two deck sections, called leaves, that pivot upward to let boats pass. Tower Bridge in London and many city drawbridges are bascule bridges.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a drawbridge?",
        "How does a lifting bridge open?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a bascule bridge have a counterweight?",
      "a": "The counterweight sits on the other side of the pivot and balances the heavy leaf. Because the two nearly cancel, the motor only has to overcome friction, wind and a small imbalance, not lift the whole leaf.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the counterweight do?",
        "Why are bascule bridges easy to lift?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a trunnion?",
      "a": "A trunnion is the large axle the leaf pivots on. It carries the weight of the leaf and counterweight while letting them rotate smoothly.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the leaf pivot on?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What powers a bascule bridge?",
      "a": "Usually electric motors or hydraulic rams. Motors turn a pinion gear against a curved rack on the leaf. Tower Bridge first used steam-driven hydraulics and changed to oil and electric power in the 1970s.",
      "asks": [
        "How is Tower Bridge powered?",
        "What makes the bridge move?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why build a bridge that opens?",
      "a": "In a flat city, a fixed bridge high enough for tall ships would need very long ramps. A moving bridge can sit low over the water and open only when a ship comes.",
      "asks": [
        "Why not just build a higher bridge?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Tower Bridge in London opened in 1894, and its bascules were first raised by hydraulic power from steam engines.",
    "The word bascule comes from the French for seesaw."
  ],
